From aa246b9d230dfebc153bf69c4fe0c8a4f0bf8a0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:30:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 051/106] mmc: pwrseq_simple: Fall back to GPIOs when no reset
 controller can be made

With a single reset GPIO, pwrseq_simple asks the reset core for a reset
controller wrapping it. The reset-gpio helper only handles #gpio-cells =
<2> and returns -ENOENT for anything else (e.g. Allwinner, <3>), which made
the probe fail, although the driver can drive the GPIO itself. Treat
-ENOENT as "no reset controller" and use the GPIO path.

The scope is narrow: with a single reset GPIO and CONFIG_RESET_GPIO
enabled, -ENOENT only comes from __reset_add_reset_gpio_device()
refusing a specifier that is not two cells; a missing property or
CONFIG_RESET_GPIO=n already give NULL. Without CONFIG_RESET_GPIO the
driver uses the GPIO directly anyway, so this restores that behaviour
for 3-cell GPIO controllers instead of failing the probe. Teaching
reset-gpio to translate other specifiers (through the GPIO chip's
of_xlate) would be the more general fix; it touches the reset core and
is left for later.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
---
 drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
index 4b47e6c3b..0e25fc957 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
@@ -137,7 +137,13 @@ static int mmc_pwrseq_simple_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	ngpio = of_count_phandle_with_args(dev->of_node, "reset-gpios", "#gpio-cells");
 	if (ngpio == 1) {
 		pwrseq->reset_ctrl = devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared(dev, NULL);
-		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl))
+		/*
+		 * -ENOENT: the GPIO cannot be wrapped as a reset controller
+		 * (e.g. #gpio-cells = <3>), drive it directly below.
+		 */
+		if (PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl) == -ENOENT)
+			pwrseq->reset_ctrl = NULL;
+		else if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl))
 			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl),
 					     "reset control not ready\n");
 	}
